WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
An allele is common on an island and rare on the mainland.

2
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.500
Did something favour it there, or was it luck?

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.160
Or did migrants bring it?

4
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.730
Picture one generation's gene pool as a bag of beads.

5
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.330
Gold beads carry one allele, and blue beads carry the other.

6
00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:24.710
Half of them are gold.

7
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:31.293
To make the next generation, a hand draws beads at random, and puts each one back

8
00:00:31.293 --> 00:00:32.433
after noting its colour.

9
00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:36.693
A small draw is rarely a perfect copy of the bag.

10
00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:39.553
This one came out heavy on gold.

11
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:42.023
Nobody chose gold.

12
00:00:43.303 --> 00:00:44.643
The sample was just small.

13
00:00:46.003 --> 00:00:50.683
Change in allele frequency from chance sampling of parents is called genetic drift.

14
00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:53.723
It needs no difference in fitness.

15
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.473
One bag tells you little.

16
00:00:57.713 --> 00:01:00.913
So run many bags side by side, all starting half gold.

17
00:01:02.293 --> 00:01:04.293
Each bag is a replicate population.

18
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:09.627
Generation after generation, each bag wanders on its own.

19
00:01:10.807 --> 00:01:12.087
Some climb and some fall.

20
00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:16.507
A few reach all gold or all blue, and then they stay there.

21
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:19.107
Now watch the white triangle.

22
00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:22.487
It marks the average across all the bags.

23
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:27.487
The bags spread apart, but the triangle stays close to where it started.

24
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.080
That is drift's signature across replicates.

25
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.740
It spreads them apart without pushing their average.

26
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.420
Chance favours no direction, so the triangle only wobbles.

27
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.830
Now make every bag ten times bigger, and start again.

28
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.450
The dots still spread, but far more slowly.

29
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.130
Bigger pools scatter less.

30
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:55.293
What sets the scatter is not simply the number of animals you count.

31
00:01:56.473 --> 00:02:01.933
It is the effective population size, the size of an ideal population that drifts as

32
00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:02.933
fast.

33
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:05.593
Back to small bags, all half gold.

34
00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:10.573
Now pour beads from outside into every bag, mostly blue ones.

35
00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:12.813
That is gene flow.

36
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.153
Every bag started at one half and got the same pour.

37
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:21.513
So every dot slides the same distance toward the immigrants.

38
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:24.453
The triangle slides with them.

39
00:02:25.653 --> 00:02:28.793
Unlike drift, this happens the same way every time.

40
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:34.387
Last, let gold carriers leave slightly more offspring, in every bag.

41
00:02:35.547 --> 00:02:37.147
That is consistent selection.

42
00:02:38.427 --> 00:02:39.727
What happens to the triangle?

43
00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.367
And what happens to the spread?

44
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.690
The triangle creeps toward gold, so consistent selection pushes the average.

45
00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:58.570
But while both alleles remain, the dots keep scattering around it.

46
00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:05.533
Here is the test. Compare the replicate average with what chance alone would give.

47
00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:10.233
Then, for selection, check whether carriers really do better.

48
00:03:11.473 --> 00:03:13.173
For gene flow, track the migrants.

49
00:03:14.283 --> 00:03:18.643
One population's frequency, on its own, cannot tell luck from cause.

50
00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:22.223
Replicates give it an average to compare with.
